WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:00.700
Good morning, John.

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:01.600
I don't know.

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:02.469
Say it with me.

00:00:02.469 --> 00:00:04.090
I don't know.

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:09.660
Good god! It is liberating! I am a towering
mountain of ignorance! I DON'T KNOW!

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:14.349
Why is a plumber named Mario? Mario, the most
well-known video game character in existence?

00:00:14.349 --> 00:00:15.179
I don't know.

00:00:15.179 --> 00:00:16.790
Why are guitars shaped like this?

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:17.630
[Hank makes weird noise]

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:18.840
Why is Iggy Azalea popular?

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.680
Good lord, I have no idea.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:24.290
I think that I know a lot of things, though
when I play Pub Trivia with my friends, I'm

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:25.320
surprisingly unhelpful.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:30.360
But, the vast majority of things, VAAAAST
majority, I don't know.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:34.390
And not just because of the sheer number of
things, but also the number of things that

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:35.190
affect those things.

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:38.489
We live in an age where we are taught to believe
that everything has a reason.

00:00:38.489 --> 00:00:43.489
And so we observe the world, we see what happened,
and then we define the thing that happened

00:00:43.489 --> 00:00:44.750
as the reason the thing happened.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:49.079
Like we say, Harry Potter's popular because,
uh, it took place in a school and it was well-written

00:00:49.079 --> 00:00:52.410
and we had a deep and unknown desire for silly
wizards.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:56.579
Which is the exact same thing as saying, "Harry
Potter's popular because it's Harry Potter."

00:00:56.579 --> 00:01:01.390
And then we think we solved it, and then when
other books about wizards and schools that

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:04.890
are well-written come out, we're like well,
it's not Harry Potter.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:08.790
Yeah, so it doesn't have all the attributes
of Harry Potter, so it's not as popular as

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:10.420
Harry Potter. We're really good at this.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:14.820
Now, I'm glad that we have the desire to understand
the world. That results in all sorts of great

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:14.960
stuff.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:19.049
We wanna know everything. We're humans. We're
curious. And maybe it's our culture, maybe

00:01:19.049 --> 00:01:19.850
it's just humanity.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:24.810
But, I think a lot of the time we end up mixing
up thinking something with knowing something.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:28.770
Suddenly, it's kinda expected that everyone
in the world has an opinion on how to solve

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:33.890
the Syrian Civil War and how to create more
jobs in the United States and "What are the

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:37.049
effects of cell phone use on the youth of
today?"

00:01:37.049 --> 00:01:41.189
Now, I know that I don't know, but somehow
everyone else seems to know.

00:01:41.189 --> 00:01:44.350
They all know differently from each other,
but they all seem to know.

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:47.810
When you look at all deeply at this, you realize
that people aren't basing their opinions on

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:51.790
what they think is the best course of action
or the actual best explanation for it. They're

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:55.520
basing it on their values, and that's fine.
It's what values are for, and values are great.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:58.210
But it might be a little silly to think that
you've discovered the best possible course

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:02.030
of action just because you figured out a way
to turn your values into executable vision.

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:06.799
Now, these guesses are useful, tremendously
useful. Weeeeeeee-eeeeee! [laughs]

00:02:06.799 --> 00:02:10.550
We convert our amalgamated opinions into,
like, reality.

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:13.940
That's the terrifying beauty of culture, and
more specifically democracy.

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:16.060
But, they are just guesses.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:21.250
No one knows the correct course of action.
We will never take it because there are too

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:22.380
many options.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:27.020
What I'm saying is nobody's opinions are correct.
In the world.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:30.670
And yet, it's impossible not to tie your opinions
to your concept of self.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:35.000
And often, people tie those things so closely
together that they begin defending their guesses

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.190
as if they're defending their very lives.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:38.280
In a way, they are.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:42.480
This is why it can be so impossible to talk
about certain topics with certain people.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:47.349
They've tied those suppositions to themselves
so tightly with knots of narrative and constructed

00:02:47.349 --> 00:02:50.790
reality and values that there's just no untying
it.

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:55.000
And maybe, unsurprisingly, in those situations
the best course of action is just to be friends.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:59.300
Maybe even ask them about that thing that
they've created because, to them, as it is

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.760
to all of us, it's immensely valuable.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:06.239
The world as we perceive it, as we've built
it inside of ourselves, is a lie that we tell

00:03:06.239 --> 00:03:10.769
to ourselves, not out of deception, but out
of necessity. We have no other choice. We

00:03:10.769 --> 00:03:16.650
simply cannot understand the world as it is.
And so, we construct, and it's a useful construction.

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:21.879
And it allows us to do many amazing things,
but sometimes I just have to tell myself the

00:03:21.879 --> 00:03:28.120
thing that is definitely true, the truest
things I can say, which is that I don't know.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:34.819
I don't know. I don't know. John, I'll see
you on Tuesday.

